# Copy to .prod.env — credentials for the PRODUCTION Cloudflare account.
# Read by deploy.sh --prod only; dev keeps using `wrangler login`.
#
# Credentials ONLY. The project name is not here — deploy.sh fixes it (prod is
# `sammaditthi`, dev is `sammaditthi-dev`) so the account and the project it
# deploys into cannot drift apart. This file just says which account.
#
# Prod lives in a separate Cloudflare account (wisdom.ops) so it can be handed
# over on its own. These two variables select that account non-interactively.
# wrangler 4.x can also hold several named login sessions (`--profile`), so this
# is not the only way to reach a second account — but it IS the way CI reaches
# it, and the GitHub Action will set the same two names as repo secrets, so
# using it by hand keeps the two paths identical.
#
# TOKEN — create an ACCOUNT-OWNED token, not a user one:
#   ops dashboard -> Manage Account -> Account API Tokens -> Create Token
#                 -> Create Custom Token
#   Permissions (search the picker for each):
#     Cloudflare Pages   Edit    <- creates and deploys projects
#     Cloudflare Pages   Read
#     Account Settings   Read    <- REQUIRED, not optional: without it
#                                   `wrangler whoami` cannot list accounts, and
#                                   deploy.sh refuses to release to production
#                                   against an account it could not verify.
#   Scope: Entire Account.  Client IP filtering: leave empty (a home IP rotates
#   and the resulting 403 is very hard to read).  TTL: an expiry is cheap — the
#   failure is a clear auth error, not silent breakage.
#
#   Account-owned (`cfat_`) rather than user-owned (`cfut_`) because the token
#   must outlive whichever person configured it — the same reason prod has its
#   own account. A user token dies with the user's access.
#
#   Do NOT use the "Edit Cloudflare Workers" template: it does not reliably
#   carry the Pages permission, and `Cloudflare Pages: Edit` is account-scoped
#   with no per-project narrowing, so this token can deploy or delete ANY Pages
#   project in the ops account. That is exactly why dev is a different account.
#
# ACCOUNT ID — 32 hex characters, and the easiest source is the dashboard URL:
#   https://dash.cloudflare.com/<ACCOUNT_ID>/workers-and-pages
#
# This file is sourced by bash, so the token need not sit here in the clear —
# a lookup works just as well and keeps the secret out of any file an editor or
# backup tool watches:
#   CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN=$(security find-generic-password -s wisdom-cf-prod -w)
CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN=
CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID=
